Key judgment calls worth flagging:
- Units 2, 5, 8: rejected candidates that split an already-short (< 120 char) sentence at a technically-valid boundary (semicolon/"so") — unnecessary fragmentation when the whole sentence fits on one line.
- Unit 3: kept "It" attached to the rejoined line rather than pushing it to a third line, per the bounded-repair rule — even though grammatically a clean split would isolate it.
- Unit 4: the checker only flagged `fused` here, but the resulting long sentence still needed a real-boundary split (I picked the colon over the original mid-clause "lookup" break, which isn't a recognized boundary).
- Unit 6: no candidate cleanly separates all four sentences without either an unneeded comma-split or a leftover fused pair, so I reported the correct 4-line split under `missing`.
